      "units": [
        {
          "id": "eks-primer-01",
          "slide": 1,
          "text": "Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service, or E K S, is managed Kubernetes on A W S. Let's follow an orders app from deployment to its first request.",
          "text_sha256": "161a09432364b4f70aa84e4da508ce96e58408ee0a7329c0b871bd72572571e8",
          "canonical_text_sha256": "2d9ba86e043544e2f8eacee6d3c23f72aad3360d05a7358626e03b6f11bc624b",
          "asset_sha256": "f4d1a39551056ab9035ca5780f48a70ccf2fd3c6a4f608c6aec486f4528488c2"
        },
        {
          "id": "eks-primer-02",
          "slide": 2,
          "text": "A developer applies a Deployment that asks Kubernetes to run two copies. The A P I server accepts the request. A W S runs that control plane in a separate A W S-managed V P C. Your app does not run there.",
          "text_sha256": "b634f9570eda30f44ce42bda330d5fc3aac56f3308204ab318beb80000488398",
          "canonical_text_sha256": "8d80a6ddea7c356e5fbb9a4c3aebf667c965bef18f24ee6f0f476518b39f3775",
          "asset_sha256": "f4d1a39551056ab9035ca5780f48a70ccf2fd3c6a4f608c6aec486f4528488c2"
        },
        {
          "id": "eks-primer-03",
          "slide": 3,
          "text": "In your V P C, E C two worker nodes sit in private subnets across two Availability Zones. The Kubernetes scheduler places the Pods onto those nodes. Here, each zone runs one copy.",
          "text_sha256": "ee20f7bc84391272c7933eb92d81f09b44bf63824a603f195eaea765001f8f63",
          "canonical_text_sha256": "51c3efad3cb00a56f112c416c276f333a655daa013a352c073fa0ffaa4e8b543",
          "asset_sha256": "f4d1a39551056ab9035ca5780f48a70ccf2fd3c6a4f608c6aec486f4528488c2"
        },
        {
          "id": "eks-primer-04",
          "slide": 4,
          "text": "The app has a Kubernetes Service. An Ingress tells the A W S Load Balancer Controller how to configure a public Application Load Balancer. H T T P S comes in through the load balancer in the public subnets.",
          "text_sha256": "8f021ee979a9888cb146ad4624a7a4b91a58fa719bb679bab69e56858c04abea",
          "canonical_text_sha256": "a7eb245a3dd50f8daf75b250b392c25ff54b2ea3469e1494119db74fef909795",
          "asset_sha256": "f4d1a39551056ab9035ca5780f48a70ccf2fd3c6a4f608c6aec486f4528488c2"
        },
        {
          "id": "eks-primer-05",
          "slide": 5,
          "text": "With I P targets, the load balancer sends requests straight to healthy Pod IPs. It doesn't send customer traffic through the Kubernetes A P I server.",
          "text_sha256": "06a187316001a18dd63a3775a73874ef4bbd29ac79109b96b6de43f85a733ac6",
          "canonical_text_sha256": "70d0da3200c75e198c1ab4b9effda5dfeee50d6282e69d52d6e1abe91aa8d76a",
          "asset_sha256": "f4d1a39551056ab9035ca5780f48a70ccf2fd3c6a4f608c6aec486f4528488c2"
        },
        {
          "id": "eks-primer-06",
          "slide": 6,
          "text": "If Pod B disappears, Kubernetes creates a replacement. It isn't ready immediately. The healthy Pod can keep serving, and the replacement can receive traffic once it passes readiness and load balancer health checks.",
          "text_sha256": "261586d13584e4e0eeed54cc975f6713d623a562f44411069926c6d5ee86657f",
          "canonical_text_sha256": "261586d13584e4e0eeed54cc975f6713d623a562f44411069926c6d5ee86657f",
          "asset_sha256": "f4d1a39551056ab9035ca5780f48a70ccf2fd3c6a4f608c6aec486f4528488c2"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
